## Conversation
The user requested router-owned Mooncake cache lifetime safety as part of the implementation, then asked for separate logical commits. A constructor-failure regression exposed an existing ownership cycle that prevented that guarantee.

## Actions
Replaced the scheduler-owned strong request-lease observer with a wrapper holding Weak<RequestLeaseManagerInner>. Router and active request leases continue to own the manager strongly. Added live admission, progress, completion, and post-manager callback coverage, plus an actual failed-router-constructor release regression for both local indexers. The constructor test passed in 0.04 seconds after reproducing the failure; the live-callback test passed in 0.01 seconds. The final integrated suite passed 189 tests, including request-lease, sequence, scheduler, and construction-failure coverage. Formatting and whitespace checks passed.

## Errors & Mistakes
The constructor test initially timed out after 2.07 seconds. Diagnostics showed threads=1, cache=1, watch=1, admitted=1, configs=2 while waiting for release. The initial assumption that the existing constructor cancellation guard was sufficient did not account for an actor retaining its own command sender. Commit preparation also rejected an overlong body line with body's lines must not be longer than 100 characters [body-max-line-length]; the shorter message passed the validator.

## Dead Ends
Cancelling only the new cache tasks cannot release a pre-existing scheduler actor reference cycle. No scheduler queue, scoring, accounting, or cleanup algorithm change was needed; weakening only the installed observer ownership resolves the cycle while preserving live callbacks.

## Hints for Future Agents
The scheduler actor owns slots and their installed observer. A strong lease-manager observer leads through SchedulerBookingCleanup back to a strong admission command sender. Keep this observer weak. Deferred admission callbacks must still replay when the observer is installed, and callbacks after manager destruction must be harmless.

## Codebase Discoveries
The failed-constructor path creates both the local indexer and scheduler before rejecting missing source membership. That path exposed retention of the passive cache and watch receivers through the scheduler actor, not through a started Mooncake subscriber.

